A||||13||SCCA FIERO||Removing powder coating?||||1||
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||12-03-2001||05:38 PM||||I tried paint stripper... didn't even phase the stuff. I don't want to use sandpaper because I think it would take forever. Not to mention I don't think I can get in all the tight places.<P>Any thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pyric||12-03-2001||05:58 PM||||Your cleaning the intake Manifold aren't you?<P>I'm still working on mine.  That factory paint takes about 10-20 applications of remover. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Pain in the...||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||12-03-2001||06:23 PM||||The best stuff I found was at Walmart and was called aircraft paint remover. I can't remember the brand name, but it was in the automotive section, came in a quart can for about $6 or $7 and had a picture of a plane on the front. <P>It took two or three applications, but it cleanly removed ALL the powder coat from my 15" diamond spoke wheels. I can't imagine anything on an intake being any tougher to remove. I tried a couple of other strippers I got at the hardware store (for $15-$20 a gallon), they took the clearcoat off but had little or no affect on the black spokes.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||12-03-2001||06:33 PM||||cant you melt it off?  <P>i never tried it but it does have a baking temp of 300.<P>there would probably a lot of fumes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||12-03-2001||06:45 PM||||Actually I am removing coating from a set of wheels. <P>I used that exact same remover (with the airplane on it) with no results.<P>If my car were a different color, these rims would look OK like they are. They're a set of those Centerline wheels with the 8 round holes and the rivets (2 piece rims). If my car wasn't red, these yellow rims (ketchup and mustard!!) might look a little better.||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||12-03-2001||06:54 PM||||I stripped mine with two applications (first one almost took it all off) of Klean-Strip Premium Stripper. It is very strong and smells very strong (don't inhale!!!). DO NOT get it on your skin, it will burn like heck. Wear rubber gloves. It can be sprayed on or painted on. I did mine in 40 degree weather outside, but it should work better if you can do it in a warmer location (well ventilated of course). It will remove paint, epoxy, or polyurethane. I got it at the Home Depot.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||JD86GT350||12-03-2001||07:20 PM||||Aircraft Remover is made by Kleen-Strip, and is some of the most caustic stuff you can get.  If you had the aerosol, try the regular can, it works slightly better, but powder coating is made <B>not</B> to come off.<P>Our wheel plant uses an acidic solution at about 200 F to help soften the powder coat, and then they're blasted.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bazooka||12-03-2001||07:30 PM||||I had my F.I. System Plastic Bead Blasted Clean. The parts came out like new.||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||12-03-2001||08:39 PM||||Take them to a powdercoating shop (they have a reagent that will weaken the coating, typically an insanely strong acid, like 12molar HFL.) Then they bead blast them.<P>They'll do it right, and you can get them to coat the wheels in clear after they're done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||rogergarrison||12-04-2001||07:25 AM||||i use the stripper (with the airplane) after the first coat, i use a wire brush to scratch up the surface, the stripper bites rite into the fine scratches and starts bubbling the paint off.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lou||12-04-2001||07:29 AM||||re-spray them with the paint color you want over the powder coat.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eriamjh||12-04-2001||07:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>cant you melt it off? <BR>i never tried it but it does have a baking temp of 300.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Since powder coating is cured with high temp (300+F like you said) it won't come off with heat.  <P>Not unless you try 1000 degrees and even that might not do it.<P>I was gonna suggest sandblasting, but I see that that has already been mentioned.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||tesmith66||12-04-2001||09:43 AM||||Dynamite  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I just painted right over mine. I did take the valve covers to an automotive machine shop and they jet cleaned them like they do intakes and tranny cases, etc. Looked like they were fresh from the foundry. Only cost $7.00.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000013||SCCA FIERO||12-04-2001||05:00 PM||||I would paint right over the coating, but it has many bubbles in it.<P>Thanks for the advice you guys!||||reg||1
